index ad06f04c4..45feb9ae3 100644
--- a/Servex/doc/processes.md
+++ b/Servex/doc/processes.md
@@ -251,6 +251,15 @@ The Bash tool's shell, grep, node, a Playwright browser, a worktree dev server:
 
 `node Servex/leaks.mjs` (a leak = a start line with no end line) reads every start and end line from the session logs and the `spawns` log and puts each unended start in a group: **gone, end missed** (the pid is not running: a logging bug), **alive, owner done** (its agent is stopped or dormant, or its task has landed: a real leak) or **alive, owner working** (fine). `--write <file>` writes the markdown report. It never kills anything. Run it, then fix the close path that missed the process (`Lifecycle.js`, `closeout`, `Pool.js`, `Server/Browser.mjs`), never the process itself. Tests: `Servex/leaks.test.mjs` (the pairing), `Servex/chrome-pin.test.mjs` (no ending path ever picks the owner's Chrome, Code or Figma; a Playwright chrome is picked; a forced kill writes its end lines).
 
+### Coverage, and the two gaps that log nothing
+
+`node Servex/pid-coverage.mjs [--hours 24]` sorts every start line in the window by the exe actually spawned (`claude.exe`, `node`, `git`, `powershell/bash`, `chrome (playwright)`, `other`) and prints, per kind, how many got an end line and how many carry both an `agent` and a `session`. Read-only, reuses `pair()`/`read_lines()` from `leaks.mjs`. A snapshot (2026-11-08, 24 h, 1,460 starts): claude.exe and chrome end and attribute near 100%; git, node and the rest end near 99% but are attributed only through their nearest tracked ancestor (an agent's own claude.exe), so a line copied to no agent's log (a lone `spawns` poll) shows 0% there by design, not as a bug.
+
+Two gaps the coverage number cannot see, because nothing is ever written for them:
+
+1. **Under 10 seconds, Lineage never samples it.** Measured the same day: about 150 shell starts were sampled in 24 h (Lineage, `"seen":"sample"`, kind `powershell/bash`), against roughly 3,280 Bash/PowerShell tool calls made by agents in the same window (counted from the transcripts' own `"name":"Bash"` / `"name":"PowerShell"` tool-use lines, the cheaper source — `~/.claude/projects/<dir>/*.jsonl` modified in the last 24 h, `grep -oc` for the two tool names). **About 1 in 22 is ever seen.** It cannot leak (gone before the next sample), so this is a visibility gap, not a leak risk.
+2. **A `claude -p` launched outside `spawn_agent` is invisible for its whole life.** `Servex/agents/roles/mastermind.md`'s "Fallback when Servex is down" recipe starts a bare `claude.exe` that Servex never put in its `owners` map, so Lineage's `root()` walk can never name it (while Servex is actually down, this is also moot — nothing is sampling at all). The bug was a mastermind using that same recipe for an ordinary `spawn_agent`/`take_worktree` **refusal** (no pool worktree ready), where Servex is up and the right move is to retry or queue. Two sessions did this on 2026-11-07/08 (`73e88e75`, `d2507b09`; a third, `00c47a44`, also used it and registered its own task folder by running `TaskDirs.mjs` itself, but still has no pid line); both had already ended by the time this was checked, and `pid-coverage.mjs`'s companion check of every live `claude.exe` against every logged pid found none unlogged right now. **Fixed 2026-11-08:** the role readme now restricts the recipe to Servex being genuinely unreachable and says to retry/queue on a plain refusal (`mastermind.md`, "Spawning").
+
 Finding things: [How to find things](/framework/ai/sessions/#how-to-find-things).
 
 Limits:
